<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<worksheet xml:id="wksht_other_trig_functions"  xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
        <title>Other Trigonometric Functions</title>
<introduction xml:id="other_trig" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
        <p>
                In addition to sine (<m>\sin(\theta)</m>) and cosine (<m>\cos(\theta)</m>) there are four other trigonometric functions, each based on one or both or sine and cosine. They are:
                <dl>
                        <li><title>Tangent</title><p><m>\tan(\theta)=\frac{\sin(\theta)}{\cos(\theta)}</m></p></li>
                        <li><title>Co-Tangent</title><p><m>\cot(\theta)=\frac{\cos(\theta)}{\sin(\theta)}</m></p></li>
                        <li><title>Secant</title><p><m>\sec(\theta)=\frac{1}{\cos(\theta)}</m></p></li>
                        <li><title>Co-Secant</title><p><m>\csc(\theta)=\frac{1}{\sin(\theta)}</m></p></li>
                </dl>
        </p>
        <p>
                        In terms of the unit circle, recall that <m>\cos(\theta)</m> is the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the point, let's call it <m>P</m>, on the unit circle at an angle <m>\theta</m> counter-clockwise from the point <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>\sin(\theta)</m> it the <m>y</m>-coordinate of <m>P</m>. (We will denote the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-coordinates of <m>P</m> by <m>x_{P}</m> and <m>y_{P}</m>, so we can say <m>\cos(\theta)=x_{P}</m> and <m>\sin(\theta)=y_{P}</m>.)
        </p>
        <p>
                        With this in mind, we see that
                <md>
                        <mrow>\tan(\theta)&amp;=\frac{\sin(\theta)}{\cos(\theta)}</mrow>
                        <mrow>&amp;=\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}},</mrow>
                </md>
                        so <m>\tan(\theta)</m> can be thought of as the slope of the line through <m>(0,0)</m> and <m>P</m>.
        </p>
                 <figure xml:id='fig_tan_unit_circle'>
                        <caption></caption>
                        <image width='75%'>
                                <description><p>The Unit Circle showing a point at angle <m>\theta</m>.</p></description>
                                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure_tan_unit_circle" >
                                        <diagram dimensions="(600, 500)" margins="5">
                                          <definition>O=(0,0)</definition>
                                          <definition>Z=(1,0)</definition>
                                          <definition>P=(-4/5,3/5)</definition>
                                          <definition>Y1=(-2,3/5)</definition>
                                          <definition>Y2=(2,3/5)</definition>
                                          <definition>X1=(-4/5,-2)</definition>
                                          <definition>X2=(-4/5,2)</definition>
                                          <definition>PE=3*P</definition>
                                          <definition>N=-3*P</definition>
                                          <coordinates bbox="(-3,-2.5,3,2.5)">
                                                  <axes />
                                                  <line endpoints="(O,P)" />
                                                  <line endpoints="(N,PE)" stroke="gray" dash="4 4"/>
                                                  <line endpoints="(X1,X2)" stroke="gray" dash="4 4"/>
                                                  <line endpoints="(Y1,Y2)" stroke="gray" dash="4 4"/>
                                                  <circle center="O" radius="1" />
                                                  <angle-marker points="(Z,O,P)" radius="40" arrow="yes">
                                                    <m>\theta</m>
                                                  </angle-marker>
                                                  <point p="P" alignment="sw" offset="(-3,-1)"><m>P</m></point>
                                                  <point p="Y2" size="0" alignment="e"><m>\sin(\theta)=y</m></point>
                                                  <point p="X2" size="0" alignment="n"><m>\cos(\theta)=x</m></point>
                                                  <point p="PE" size="0" alignment="n"><m>\tan(\theta)=\frac{y}{x}</m></point>
                                          </coordinates>
                                        </diagram>
                                        </prefigure>
                        </image>
        </figure>
</introduction>
